  "problem": "A research module of mass $20\\,\\text{kg}$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the module's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the module is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the module\u2019s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the module are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}",
